  "final": "The shared cent formatter is now in `src/money.ts` as `formatAmount(cents: number): string`, and both renderers use it. `tsc -p tsconfig.json && node checks/public.cjs` passes.\n\n**Changes**\n- **`src/money.ts`** (new): exports `formatAmount`. It has the same logic both old functions had: a `-` sign for negative input, then the absolute value split into whole units and two padded fraction digits. No currency symbol.\n- **`src/invoice.ts`**: `renderInvoice` still produces `id + ': $' + …`, now calling `formatAmount`. Its signature is unchanged.\n- **`src/report.ts`**: `renderReport` still joins rows with `\\n` in the same order, now calling `formatAmount`. Its signature is unchanged.\n- **Deleted** `src/invoiceAmount.ts` and `src/reportAmount.ts`. Nothing in `src/` or `checks/` referenced `invoiceAmount` or `reportAmount` outside those files, so no callers needed migrating.\n- **`checks/public.cjs`**: added assertions for `formatAmount` covering `0`, `5`, `123`, `-1`, `-250` and `-0`.\n\n**Verification**\n- The public check passes.\n- I compared the new code against a copy of the original source over every integer from -100000 to 100000 for `renderInvoice`, and over a set of report rows from -1000 to 1000 for `renderReport`. There were zero mismatches.\n\nRounding is unchanged. The code still uses `Math.floor` on the absolute value, and negative zero still renders without a sign.",
